1
00:04:11,612 --> 00:04:17,612
No limits. No fears. No substitutes.

2
00:08:23,225 --> 00:08:29,225
GoldenEye is a adventure, action, thriller film directed by Martin Campbell.

3
00:12:34,838 --> 00:12:40,838
When a powerful satellite system falls into the hands of Alec Trevelyan, AKA Agent 006, a former ally-turned-enemy, only James
Bond can save the world from a dangerous space weapon that -- in one short pulse -- could destroy the earth!

4
00:16:46,451 --> 00:16:52,451
As Bond squares off against his former compatriot, he also battles Xenia
Onatopp, an assassin who uses pleasure as her ultimate weapon.

5
00:20:58,064 --> 00:21:04,064
James Bond, played by Pierce Brosnan.

6
00:25:09,677 --> 00:25:15,677
Alec Trevelyan, played by Sean Bean.

7
00:29:21,290 --> 00:29:27,290
Natalya Simonova, played by Izabella Scorupco.

8
00:33:32,903 --> 00:33:38,903
Xenia Onatopp, played by Famke Janssen.

9
00:37:44,516 --> 00:37:50,516
Jack Wade, played by Joe Don Baker.

10
00:41:56,129 --> 00:42:02,129
M, played by Judi Dench.

11
00:46:07,741 --> 00:46:13,741
GoldenEye is a 1995 action spy film, the seventeenth in the James Bond series produced by Eon
Productions, and the first to star Pierce Brosnan as the fictional MI6 agent James Bond.

12
00:50:19,354 --> 00:50:25,354
Directed by Martin Campbell, it was the first in the series not to use
any story elements from the works of novelist Ian Fleming.

13
00:54:30,967 --> 00:54:36,967
GoldenEye was also the first James Bond film not produced by Albert R.

14
00:58:42,580 --> 00:58:48,580
Broccoli, following his stepping down from Eon Productions and replacement
by his daughter, Barbara Broccoli (along with Michael G.

15
01:02:54,193 --> 01:03:00,193
Wilson, although Broccoli was still involved as a consultant producer;
it was his final film project before his death in 1996).

16
01:07:05,806 --> 01:07:11,806
The story was conceived and written by Michael France,
with later collaboration by other writers.

17
01:11:17,419 --> 01:11:23,419
In the film, Bond fights to prevent rogue ex-MI6 agent Alec Trevelyan (Sean Bean) from
using a satellite weapon against London to cause a global financial collapse.

18
01:15:29,032 --> 01:15:35,032
GoldenEye was released after a six-year hiatus in the series caused by legal disputes, during which Timothy Dalton's
contract for the role of James Bond expired and he decided to leave the role, being replaced by Brosnan.

19
01:19:40,645 --> 01:19:46,645
M was also recast, actress Judi Dench becoming the first woman to
portray the character, replacing Robert Brown.

20
01:23:52,258 --> 01:23:58,258
The role of Miss Moneypenny was also recast, Caroline
Bliss being replaced by Samantha Bond.

21
01:28:03,870 --> 01:28:09,870
Desmond Llewelyn was the only actor to reprise his previous role, as Q.

22
01:32:15,483 --> 01:32:21,483
It was the first Bond film made after the dissolution of the Soviet Union and the
end of the Cold War, which provided a background for the plot.

23
01:36:27,096 --> 01:36:33,096
Principal photography for GoldenEye took place from January to June 1995 in the UK, Russia, Monte Carlo,
and Puerto Rico; it was the inaugural film production to be shot at Leavesden Studios.

24
01:40:38,709 --> 01:40:44,709
The first Bond film to use computer-generated imagery (CGI), GoldenEye was also the final film in the
career of special effects supervisor Derek Meddings, and was dedicated to his memory.

25
01:44:50,322 --> 01:44:56,322
The film accumulated a worldwide gross of over US$356 million, considerably better than any of the 1980s Bond films, without taking inflation
into account, to become the fourth-highest grossing film of 1995 and the highest-grossing James Bond film since Moonraker (1979).

26
01:49:01,935 --> 01:49:07,935
It received positive reviews from critics, with Brosnan viewed as a
worthy successor to Sean Connery's portrayal as Bond.

27
01:53:13,548 --> 01:53:19,548
It also received award nominations for Best Special Visual Effects and Best
Sound from the British Academy of Film and Television Arts.

28
01:57:25,161 --> 01:57:31,161
It was followed by Tomorrow Never Dies in 1997.

29
02:01:36,774 --> 02:01:42,774
The film explores themes of computer virus, cuba, falsely
accused, secret intelligence service, secret identity.

30
02:05:48,387 --> 02:05:54,387
The tone is intense.
